WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.968
-Welcome to "The Tonight Show."
What should I call you?

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:04.868
Do I call you Beeple?

00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:07.601
-You can call me Mike or Beeple.
I'm pretty "cas."

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:10.133
So whatever, either way is good.

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:11.234
-How did Beeple come about?

00:00:11.234 --> 00:00:12.868
What did it come from?

00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:15.667
-So, it's a little toy
from, like, the '80s.

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:17.834
It's this, like,
Ewok-looking thing

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:20.133
that, like, beeps
and, like, lights up.

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:21.834
It was never
really that popular.

00:00:21.834 --> 00:00:23.667
-Yeah, but you just got
the nickname Beeple.

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:24.934
Yeah. Or you just took the name?

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:26.667
-I took the name.
-You took the name.

00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:30.167
Before we get into last night,
and I'm so excited for you --

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:31.434
congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:00:31.434 --> 00:00:34.100
-I want to get into --
Let's explain what you do.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.734
You're a digital artist

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:39.400
who sells NFTs of your artwork,
is that correct?

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.501
-Yeah, so I've been
making digital art

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:43.133
for the last like 20 years

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:46.801
and sort of growing in sort of
popularity over that time.

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:49.033
But there was no way to
sort of collect my work

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:51.100
without the, like,
NFT part of it.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.667
And, so then this technology
came along, NFTs, that allow

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:58.434
sort of, like, ownership
over something virtual.

00:00:58.434 --> 00:01:01.367
And that allowed people to
actually collect what I do,

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.501
similar to a painting
or a sculpture

00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:05.200
or any other
sort of, like, artist.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.601
And so things have gotten
a little crazy since.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:11.434
-Yeah. So, you just found out
about NFTs?

00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:12.968
-It was about a year ago.

00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:15.801
And, so, my, like, fans kept
coming up to me and being like,

00:01:15.801 --> 00:01:16.801
"You know, you should
check this out.

00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:18.067
You really should
check this out."

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:20.133
And at first, I was like,
"I don't get this."

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.968
And then finally I did,
and then it was like,

00:01:21.968 --> 00:01:24.634
"This is going to be big.
It's going to be big."

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:27.267
-I just got my first NFT.
-Oh, you did? What was it?

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:30.067
-Through MoonPay.
-Okay, there it is.

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:31.067
There it is.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:32.100
-Know what I'm talking about?

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.734
This is real.

00:01:34.734 --> 00:01:38.701
MoonPay -- I did my homework.
-Okay.

00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:40.934
-MoonPay, which is like PayPal
but for crypto.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:44.934
And I bought an ape.

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:47.300
-Okay, yeah, a bored ape?
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.601
-I was going to say
I bought "Human One"

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:51.267
last night for $29 million.

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:53.667
-You should've, bro.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:57.634
-But before NFTs, before you did
this and to be able to do this,

00:01:57.634 --> 00:01:59.200
I think NFTs are great
for artists.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.334
But what was the largest amount
of money

00:02:01.334 --> 00:02:02.701
you sold a piece of work for?

00:02:02.701 --> 00:02:07.133
-$100, because it sort of,
like, nobody was --

00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:09.067
I wasn't trying to sell work
like that.

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:11.834
And sort of, like, and people --
you could sell prints,

00:02:11.834 --> 00:02:14.567
but it just -- generally people
didn't do it that much.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.033
They weren't as excited about it

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:19.033
versus this being sort of,
like, natively digital.

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:20.667
People are very excited
about it.

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:24.934
-Last night "Human One"
sold for $29 million.

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:26.434
-It did.

00:02:26.434 --> 00:02:28.367
-Were you there?

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:31.033
-Yeah, we were at the auction.
My whole family was there.

00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:33.501
I've got two young kids,
and my parents were there.

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:39.234
And yeah, they set us up with a
little booth and food and stuff.

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:41.801
And, yeah, it was
definitely very surreal.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:44.367
-But it's fascinating.
It's a great piece of art.

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:47.501
It's an astronaut
just continuously walking.

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:50.934
-Yeah, it's a giant metal box
that has, like,

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:53.534
a bunch of screens on it,
and it's slowly rotating.

00:02:53.534 --> 00:02:55.968
And it kind of, like,
creates this illusion.

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:57.601
-We have video footage.

00:02:57.601 --> 00:03:02.467
-Inside this
kind of, like, void.

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:04.300
So, it's like a metal --

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.200
It's a giant, like,
refrigerator-sized box

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.968
that just keeps turning,

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:10.767
and the environment
will continue to change.

00:03:10.767 --> 00:03:13.234
And I think that's what's
exciting about this piece

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:17.067
and I think is more analogous
to sort of the future of art,

00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:20.200
is that we'll have pieces in our
house that continue to change

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.601
and sort of -- it's not just a
static painting that just --

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.300
it is what it is.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.834
I think we're going to have
more things like this.

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:31.901
-Yeah. The first time I heard
about you was another auction.

00:03:31.901 --> 00:03:35.400
Your work sold for $69 million.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.767
Put you in top three
most valuable living artists

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:39.901
in the world, buddy.

00:03:39.901 --> 00:03:42.300
-It's been a year.
-It has been a year, pal.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.667
But let me tell you something --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:46.734
It's been a year, yeah.
I just love the story.

00:03:46.734 --> 00:03:50.734
I love the American dream
because, you know, you were

00:03:50.734 --> 00:03:53.534
happy before you sold --
when you sold things for $100.

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:55.067
-Yeah.
-And having a great life.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:57.934
You have a great wife, kids and
mom and dad -- I met them.

00:03:57.934 --> 00:04:03.200
And then this -- this collage,
I guess you call it.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.467
-Yeah, yeah.
-Called "Every Days,"

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:07.400
you started doing it
14 years ago.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.167
You painted every single day.

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:12.734
-Yeah, I do a picture
every single day.

00:04:12.734 --> 00:04:17.133
Started in 2007 and it's done,
start to finish that day.

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:18.200
They're mostly done --
No, they're done

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:19.601
on the computer, most of them.

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:21.767
Some of them were drawn
in the very, very beginning.

00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:23.801
But almost all of them are done
in the computer each day.

00:04:23.801 --> 00:04:26.634
-But you had no clue that
NFTs would ever exist.

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:28.767
-No. Before the blockchain.

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:32.000
That was before, like,
any of this stuff.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.434
-What is your wife
saying to you?

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:40.234
Like, "Honey, what are you doing
with all these digital paintings

00:04:40.234 --> 00:04:42.634
you -- every single day
since 2007?"

00:04:42.634 --> 00:04:44.434
-Yeah, well,
and that's the thing.

00:04:44.434 --> 00:04:46.267
Like, they had brought
in a bunch of work.

00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:48.601
Like, I've done, you know,
concert visuals.

00:04:48.601 --> 00:04:50.534
And I was sort of, you know,
growing a following

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:52.901
and getting better and better
sort of freelance work

00:04:52.901 --> 00:04:55.300
from these pictures.
-They're gorgeous. Amazing.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.667
-I appreciate that.

00:04:56.667 --> 00:04:57.701
-I want to show
a couple of these.

00:04:57.701 --> 00:04:58.901
-Oh, boy. Oh, boy.

00:04:58.901 --> 00:05:01.033
This is not -- I don't -- Okay.
That one's okay.

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:02.667
-This first one,

00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:05.167
this is different than
your other stuff, right?

00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:07.400
-Yeah, this was the --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.834
This was the absolute,
very first one.

00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:13.100
And that is a picture of
my Uncle Jim that I drew.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.000
I don't know why
I drew it that day.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.734
It was just like, "Okay,
I'm going to start this thing.

00:05:16.734 --> 00:05:19.701
Obviously could not have
imagined that, you know,

00:05:19.701 --> 00:05:22.334
14 years later now --
-It's on "The Tonight Show."

00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:24.734
-There it is.
-There you go Uncle Jim, yeah.

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:28.133
But then this is more the type
of Beeple stuff I'm like --

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:31.934
This is called "Jabba
the Pooh/Winnie the Hutt."

00:05:31.934 --> 00:05:33.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:35.033
-Okay, I don't know.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:37.501
That's, uh --
Some of them are weird.

00:05:37.501 --> 00:05:38.868
I will say
some of them are weird.

00:05:38.868 --> 00:05:40.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.501
-This one might be one of
my favorite ones.

00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:44.968
This one's called "First Emoji."
-There you go.

00:05:44.968 --> 00:05:48.467
-Our ancestors building --
or someone building Rome?

00:05:48.467 --> 00:05:50.367
Who knows?
-Yeah, I was just kind of

00:05:50.367 --> 00:05:53.234
trying to imagine if they were,
like, building this weird thing

00:05:53.234 --> 00:05:55.100
that they didn't even understand
why they were building

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.167
this emoji or what it is.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.167 --> 00:05:59.467
Back in medieval times.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:07.400
This was actually the day
my daughter was born, and --

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.367
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.367 --> 00:06:13.434
I had to do it very quickly
before we went to the hospital.

00:06:13.434 --> 00:06:15.400
[ Laughter ]
Which doesn't sound great,

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.901
but it was like two minutes,
so yeah.

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:21.634
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:21.634 --> 00:06:24.701
-And this one's
just called "Hey."

00:06:24.701 --> 00:06:28.968
[ Laughter ]

00:06:28.968 --> 00:06:30.734
Okay, yeah, self-explanatory.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.734 --> 00:06:31.767
-I think we're good there.

00:06:31.767 --> 00:06:33.100
I think we're pretty good there,
yeah.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:34.868
-Do you have any advice
for people who want to

00:06:34.868 --> 00:06:37.100
get into this or get into
what you're doing?

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.367
-Yeah, there's tons of tutorials
out there.

00:06:39.367 --> 00:06:43.000
And there's, you know,
free software out there as well.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.200
And I think it's something that,
you know,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.601
takes a lot of
work and practice.

00:06:47.601 --> 00:06:50.033
And, again, I've been, you know,
making digital art for 20 years.

00:06:50.033 --> 00:06:53.534
So it's not going to be kind of
an overnight thing.

00:06:53.534 --> 00:06:56.133
But I think you're going to see
a lot of people do this

00:06:56.133 --> 00:06:59.601
and a lot more sort of,
you know, artists kind of take

00:06:59.601 --> 00:07:02.501
this approach just because it is
another way you can kind of,

00:07:02.501 --> 00:07:04.734
you know, monetize
and sort of make a living.

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:07.000
-Congratulations on everything,
buddy. I'm a fan.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.167
Please come back.
-Thank you so much.

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:09.701
-We'll follow your career.

00:07:09.701 --> 00:07:10.801
Beeple, everybody!

